  "course_key": "g3-sec2-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec2-s1",
  "position": 4,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Mean, median and mode",
  "summary": "Calculate and interpret the three principal measures of central tendency, including missing-value problems.",
  "outcomes": [
    "1.8"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "s1-lesson-07"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 60,
  "objectives": [
    "Calculate mean, median and mode from raw data.",
    "Recover a missing value from a stated mean.",
    "Explain how ordering, frequency and magnitude affect the three measures."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "s1-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-explanation-1",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Mean uses every magnitude",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Add all values and divide by the number of observations. The mean balances the data total equally across observations. Because every magnitude contributes, one extreme value can move the mean substantially. The diagram shows the data of the second worked example: the mean, 6, is the balance point of the five dots, while the median and mode are both 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "s1-lesson-08-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-explanation-2",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Median uses ordered position",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Order the data before finding the middle. For an odd number of values, select the central value. For an even number, average the two central values. The median is resistant to extremes because it depends mainly on position."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-explanation-3",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Mode uses frequency",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The mode is the most frequent value and may be unique, multiple or absent. It can describe numerical or categorical data. Check frequencies rather than choosing a visually central value."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-example-1",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: recover a missing value",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Five values have mean 12. Four of the values total 43. Find the fifth value."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The total of all five values is the mean multiplied by the number of values."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "5\\times12=60"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "60-43=17"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "With the fifth value, the total is 43 + 17 = 60, and 60 ÷ 5 = 12."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: compare all three measures",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For the data 2, 4, 4, 7, 13 in the diagram, find the mean, median and mode."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "\\text{mean}=\\frac{2+4+4+7+13}{5}=\\frac{30}{5}=6"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The values are already in order, and the middle (third) value is 4, so the median is 4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "4 appears twice and every other value once, so the mode is 4."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The distances from the mean, −4, −2, −2, +1 and +7, add to 0, which confirms that 6 is the balance point. The value 13 pulls the mean above the median."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-recall",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Find the median of 3, 5, 8, 9, 12."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "8",
        "canonical_latex": "8",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The data are ordered and the third of five values is 8."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "s1-08-summary",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Lesson checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mean = total ÷ number of values. Median = middle value of the ordered data (the mean of the two middle values for an even count). Mode = most frequent value."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For a missing value, find the required total from the mean first. Order the data before finding the median."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
